#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# abs

#% text
# `abs(x)` gives the absolute value of the real or complex number `x`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# For a complex number `a + b*im` the absolute value is `sqrt(a^2 + b^2)`.

#% item
# Exact numbers give exact results; exact real expressions such as `1 - sqrt(2)` are resolved by their sign.

#% item
# Machine numbers and balls give numbers of the same kind.

#% item
# A symbol stays inside `abs`, since its sign is not known.

#% item
# `abs` is listable: it applies to each element of a list.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
abs(-3)
#% output
#= 3

#% cell
abs(-2.5)
#% output
#= 2.5

#% text
# The modulus of a complex number:

#% cell
abs(3 + 4im)
#% output
#= 5

#% text
# Each element of a list:

#% cell
abs([-1, 2, -3])
#% output
#= [1, 2, 3]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Exact real expressions:

#% cell
abs(-pi)
#% output
#= pi

#% cell
abs(1 - sqrt(2))
#% output
#= -1 + 2^(1/2)

#% cell
abs(sin(1) - 1)
#% output
#= 1 - sin(1)

#% text
# A complex machine number:

#% cell
abs(1.0 + 1.0*im)
#% output
#= 1.4142135623730951

#% text
# Balls:

#% cell
abs(n(1 - sqrt(2), 25))
#% output
#= 0.41421356237309504880168872421

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The derivative of `abs` for real arguments is the sign:

#% cell
d(abs(x), x)
#% output
#= sign(x)

#% text
# The absolute value of a complex number is its distance from 0, the same as the norm of the pair of its parts:

#% cell
abs(1 + im) == norm([1, 1])
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A symbolic argument stays, even when its sign seems clear:

#% cell
abs(x^2)
#% output
#= abs(x^2)

#% text
# The absolute value of an infinity is not taken:

#% cell
abs(-inf)
#% output
#= abs(-inf)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `sign`, `arg`, `re`, `im_part`, `conjugate`, `norm`
